WEBVTT

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:48.950
the icy waters off the Alaskan coast. Killer whales are on patrol. These, the most intelligent

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:54.890
hunters in the sea, are moving relentlessly towards a particular time and place.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:00.000
They hunt with phenomenal power, pursuing their prey at speeds of up to 40 miles an

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.680
pod cruises straight towards a patch of coastline where for several miles the frigid water has

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:21.230
turned a milky white. Each spring, millions of adult herring come here to spawn, producing

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:26.880
billions of eggs. But it is the herring the whales are after.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:41.730
Usually, the whales hunt alone, but with this potential catch, they work as a team, and they

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.210
pursue an ingenious strategy.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.820
First, they push the fish into the shallows.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.410
Then, they gently herd the herring together.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.850
No sudden moves, yet.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:02:00.000
Working together, the hunters bunch the fish into ever-tightening balls, signaling

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:08.030
each other on toward the crucial moment. And finally, the whales attack. Not with their

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:14.750
teeth, but with their tails. A killer whale can slap at dozens of fish at once, stunning

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:22.800
them with sheer muscle power. And the banquet begins. Each whale can scoop up as many as

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:30.000
400 herring in a day. Whenever hunters like these converge on a massive natural phenomenon,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.240
in perfect synchronicity.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.990
The stage is set for one of nature's great events.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:28.640
is a world in motion, a world of change. Currents sweep across the oceans, storms batter coastlines,

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:42.180
threatens grasslands until rain brings new life winter locks the poles in ice and spring brings

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:52.860
floods each spectacular change is a catalyst for the planet's hungry hunters there is one constant

00:03:52.860 --> 00:04:00.000
the need to find food whatever the conditions no matter the odds whether the predators hunter look

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.650
alone or in teams, they obey a powerful instinct. But when ferocious hunger clashes with the

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:20.660
will to escape, the outcome is rarely certain. This is the drama of the hunt. One of the

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:27.270
most powerful forces that triggers change is sunlight. As it falls upon the volcanic

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.000
great rift valley of East Africa, sunshine.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.810
the sunshine unleashes a surprising chain of events.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.550
Come December, the region's alkaline lakes

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.940
are bathed in more ultraviolet light

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.250
than at any other time of year.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:47.300
The water becomes thick with algae,

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.240
and soon the lakeshore fills with slender legs

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.550
and necks and beaks that sift the water

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.220
for the nutritious plants.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:09.080
Flamingos have travelled here from their breeding grounds some 500 miles due south.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.060
Between two and three million flamingos here on Lake Begoria alone.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:19.830
The lake is full of caustic natural chemicals, products of volcanic ash.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.400
The water is uninhabitable for most living things.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:30.000
The flamingos are a showy exception and a tempting target for any hunter willing to pursue them.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:39.940
One predator is this bird, among Africa's largest, the fish eagle.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.280
Another is a foreigner, the step eagle.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:50.480
Two massive birds of prey, two different approaches to the hunt.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:56.530
For the past two months, fish eagles have been feeding their hungry chicks with fish from the nearby freshwater lakes.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:14.030
These eagles are able to catch 4 or 5 large fish during a day of hunting in this peak season.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:25.790
With remarkable precision and keen eyesight, the eagle tracks its target.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.000
But by December, the local fish supply has dwindled.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:35.300
breeding season is over. The flamingos arriving at the Soda Lakes offer a much

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:42.590
bigger meal. If the fish eagle strategy relies on skill the step eagle relies on

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:48.150
strength. These eagles nest in Russia and once their breeding season comes to an

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:54.400
end they have an urgent need to flee the coming winter. As early as October the

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.980
birds begin to leave their summer home. By January the Russian steppes will be

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.000
frozen solid.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.800
The search for a regular supply of small prey draws them on a flight across the mountains

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:12.420
of Eastern Europe, into Israel, and on to Africa, 4,000 miles in all.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.590
As December turns to January, the players are in place.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:27.980
Lake Begoria is now home to 20 pairs of fish eagles, crowned in white, millions of pink

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:28.740
flamingos.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.870
And the last to arrive, dozens of brown step eagles.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.530
Three majestic species, all poised to meet at the water's edge.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:48.800
A fish eagle makes the first move,

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:51.830
searching for any stragglers amid the mass of flamingos.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:57.750
As long as the birds stay together, they present an impossible tug.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:06.840
only left alone

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:07.600
and are exposed

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:08.820
to the eagles' strike.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:29.270
The eagles circle

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:30.000
to keep the flock

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:49.250
on the move. Eventually, one bird is separated from the rest and panics into a fatal error.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:55.120
Thin neck of a flamingo is no match for the eagle's weight, talons, or seven-foot wingspan.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.950
The hunter keeps an eagle eye on its prize,

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.720
food for up to two days.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.280
But now the step eagle joins in the action,

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.160
lacking the precision of the fish eagle,

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:28.020
but packing plenty of brute force.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.000
In its swoops to steal the flamingo,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.440
literally out from under the larger bird.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.370
But the flamingo is able to stagger away.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:50.720
Neither eagle wins a meal, this time.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:53.260
With so many eagles gathered at the lake shore,

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.280
the competition for flamingos turns fierce.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:58.480
Now it's war.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.200
Both species are expert hunters.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:18.210
But over time, the steppe eagles have learned to plunder from the fish eagles.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:22.020
Now they must defend the spoils from others of their own kind.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:43.880
fish eagles retreat they'll hunt again later for the long-distance flyers the grueling trip

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:52.350
has definitely paid off south of the lakes on the plains of east africa the scent of fresh

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:59.420
rain drives vast herds of grazers to migrate towards new grass but this new source of plenty

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.000
comes at a

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:16.630
price the animals are now within range of that fearsome hunter the lion today's

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:23.770
target zebra a fully grown zebra packs twice the body weight of a lioness and

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:30.000
it's equipped with a powerful kick cats senses are

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:36.700
to wind direction, the terrain and cover. They must read the conditions, predict

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:42.690
which way their quarry will run, and avoid the flying hooves. The lionesses

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:50.870
have surrounded the zebra. Now they spring their track.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.480
This zebra will find no escape.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.800
but sometimes an animal can find the subtle edge

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.600
that tips the balance in its favor.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:50.830
Sunlight also fires a different kind of hunt, a harvest.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:56.810
These are plankton blooming in the sun-warmed sea,

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.000
tiny animals blossoming in such numbers

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.390
as to nourish some of the largest creatures in the ocean, sharks.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:17.970
These toothless giants are basking sharks, up to 40 feet long.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:22.850
They live a mysterious life in cold oceans,

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:26.690
suddenly drawn out of the darkness by the appearance of the plankton blue.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.000
Basking sharks sweep seawater.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.320
through their gills, straining it before digesting half a ton of plankton at any

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:41.180
one time. The largest creatures fueled by the ocean's smallest. In the fluid world

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:49.030
of the sea, change is underway night and day. Sunlight and shadow, warmth and cold.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:57.460
Some 600 miles off the coast of Ecuador in the South American tropics lie the

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:58.870
Galapagos Islands.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.930
Here there are predators who have only to wait for their prey to come to them.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:08.810
One of the ocean's great currents brings with it an upwelling of cold water that's like

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:15.220
a liquid freight train of fish, enough to support 15,000 penguins.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:22.130
But its arrival is highly unpredictable.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:27.550
Nowhere else on earth do penguins live so far north, far from the Antarctic's frigid

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.120
but food-rich seas.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:41.230
The Galapagos penguins must wait sometimes as long as a year for the current to sweep past,

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.310
and then feed as fast as they can.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.630
Once the adults have fed, they will lay their eggs,

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.520
and then catch more fish for their newly hatched young.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.700
The infrequent arrivals of the current makes for a precarious way of life.

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.840
Should the current fail to deliver, the penguins cannot breed.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.500
But when the current does arrive,

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:12.240
the gamble pays off.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.230
While Galapagos penguins must dive for their dinner,

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:19.600
other ocean birds take a different tack.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.820
As another ocean current hits the west coast of the Americas,

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:26.080
seagulls prepare to embark upon a life of crime.

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:38.640
They're waiting not only for the food-rich current, but for hunters who will do their work for them.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:40.430
Brown pelicans.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:52.270
As huge numbers of fish begin to arrive, the pelicans are close behind, specialists in the high dive.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:57.560
So the gulls leave the diving to the experts.

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:04.450
The technique has been perfected over the ages.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:09.760
First a controlled crash dive, equal to a plunge off a six-story building.

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:17.000
Then a quick grab of anchovies and sardines from well below the surface.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:35.790
When the first pelicans appear with their catch, the gulls scramble.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.870
Until this moment, the fish were beyond the gulls' reach.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:47.340
But now agility and sheer thievery provide a second-hand meal.

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:55.800
Gulls are relentless.

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:17.030
It's only when the pelicans tire of the pickpockets and refuse to dive again that the gulls banquet

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:22.670
ends.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:27.690
When nature repaints an entire landscape, hunters are confronted with new challenges,

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:30.000
especially far to the north, when temperatures

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:41.900
plummet and the world turns to ice. The animals of the Arctic scavenge for what

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:48.570
food remains. Male polar bears have had a hard autumn, padding through the

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:53.370
thickening blizzards in search of meat. But once the freeze takes hold, solid

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:58.090
pack ice will present a pathway to their prey. The bears prepare to head out

00:17:58.090 --> 00:17:59.640
across a frozen sea.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.080
Meanwhile, the female polar bear beds down in the snow.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:18.440
It'll be her home for the winter, perhaps even a nursery.

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:25.990
When the sun sets here, only the cold remains.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:30.000
The aurora borealis, the northern lights, streak across the night's

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.710
sky. There's no choice but to endure five months when the land is locked in ice.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:43.430
There's a rich supply of food for the Arctic's walruses and seals, but it can be hunted only

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:50.120
beneath the pack ice. The sea mammals need air to breathe, so they come and go from breaks in the

00:18:50.120 --> 00:19:00.000
ice or make their own breathing holes. The polar bear searches for these holes. Telled

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.720
tailed doorways left ajar.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.600
Polar bears, the largest

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:06.520
of all land predators, may wander

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:08.720
across 100,000 square miles

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.570
in the course of a winter, depending

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.130
on the availability of prey.

00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:18.970
A male polar bear may weigh up to

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:21.610
half a ton. It needs to eat

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:30.000
regularly, and it may be forced

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.640
to swim in pursuit of its prey.

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:34.490
Polar bears are excellent swimmers,

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:37.290
and they're insulated from the numbing cold

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.150
by a coat of hollow hairs.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.560
This bear has spotted a ringed seal on the ice

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:44.990
near its breathing hole.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:55.250
When the seal dives, the bear begins to stalk.

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:10.510
If this is the seal's only hold, it will be forced to return in order to take its next breath.

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:19.620
So the polar bear waits, waits.

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:25.550
When the seal surfaces, the bear strikes.

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:30.000
But the ambush has caught the attack.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.000
of another larger bear.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:38.110
A clash looms, and the carcass is relinquished.

00:20:38.110 --> 00:20:41.950
The lore of the Arctic prevails.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:51.740
When wintry desolation grips the land,

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:54.830
the Ida Ducks' response is to leave the ice behind

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:56.880
and take their chances on the sea.

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:17.080
of the strongest long-distance flyers of any birds.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.920
This flock is hundreds strong.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:21.500
Experience has taught the idoducks

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:25.640
where to find pockets of food in the offshore shallows.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.120
But can they reach it if it's 15 feet down?

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:36.020
The eider duck undergoes a remarkable transformation from flyer to submarine.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:40.400
Each bird powers its way down to a dense bed of mussels.

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:46.750
It takes fantastic strength for the eider ducks to work against their own buoyancy.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:53.990
They have only a few seconds to peck at the shellfish before the sea sends the ducks popping back to the surface.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:22:00.000
Mussels are not rich in energy, so the birds must dive repeatedly.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:11.400
This rare find will keep the ducks going for at least a few more days on the frigid sea.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:26.860
Last winter relinquishes its hold.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:30.000
Thousands of square miles are released from the sea.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:45.480
Arctic freeze. Along rocky ledges still covered with snow, Guillemots fly in to stake a precarious

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:51.510
claim. Their nesting is just what the Arctic fox, still wearing its thick winter coat, has been

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:57.220
waiting for. Squabbles over the best sights bring the adult birds within reach.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:07.790
And since it's all a prelude to breeding, there is also the promise of a greater bounty in the spring.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:18.440
With the coming of the thaw, polar bears can no longer hunt on the ice.

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:20.490
They head back to solid land.

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:30.000
But perhaps one year in ten, the thaw comes so early that the bears are marooned far from the mainland, on Wrangel Island, off the Siberian coast.

00:23:31.530 --> 00:23:36.120
Nature's capricious scheduling compensates the stranded bears.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:43.190
There's a bonanza on this beach.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:45.740
An entire colony of walruses,

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:48.950
lingering on the rocky shore where they've reared their young.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:07.670
for the polar bear it's a question of first finding your walrus waking up your walrus

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:13.910
and then trying very hard to sink your teeth into it while avoiding the lethal front end with its

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:21.740
foot-long tusks a walrus is covered in up to five inches of blubber and thick hide

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:30.000
the bear must be realistic it's often not the largest prize but the smallest and he carries

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.840
bear's good fortune soon spreads.

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:52.730
More polar bears invade the beach.

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:55.970
Quirk of nature can set the

00:24:55.970 --> 00:24:57.670
scene for an exceptional encounter

00:24:57.670 --> 00:25:00.000
in which one species benefits

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:13.250
at the expense of another.

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:25.350
Now the Arctic's plants and animals race to unleash their own miracles of spring.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:27.840
The Guillemot colony is bustling with chicks.

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:36.820
just getting ready to leave the nest for the very first time, and the arctic foxes, no

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:40.120
longer wearing their winter white, are gathering below.

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:50.310
The chick's maiden flight is a treacherous one.

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:54.680
They must glide hundreds of yards from the cliff to the safety of the sea, guided by

00:25:54.680 --> 00:25:56.240
their attentive parents.

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:22.400
With the foxes waiting below, any miscalculation can be fatal.

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:49.680
But many of the youngsters survived their crash landing

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:51.910
and reached the safety of the family.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:11.720
This tiny rodent is the key to survival for dozens of arctic predators.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:18.880
The lemming, breeding on its own timetable, a four-year cycle of boom and bust.

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:24.260
In some years, lemmings hardly breed at all, but in others, they breed by the million.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:38.660
This hunter is an arctic skewer.

00:27:38.660 --> 00:27:43.220
This one, a male snowy owl, uses its sharp eyesight to spot a telltale movement in the

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:49.460
tundra and end one short lemming life.

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:52.240
The snowy owl has produced young of its own.

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:56.460
The owls can breed only when there are enough lemmings to feed the growing chicks.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:00.000
How well the chicks will survive depends entirely on how many lemmings can be found.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:09.050
fed to them. There's competition for the lemmings. One arctic fox may catch dozens per day, even as

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:17.390
they are dive-bombed by the skewers which have their own families to feed. Fox cubs could go

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:24.280
hungry. Luckily, this year's lemming supply is a bumper crop. The fox population, too, will grow.

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:41.910
With predator populations booming, the Arctic still has room for one more,

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:46.710
now emerging from the ponds and lakes.

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:59.960
The tiniest of the Arctic hunters: mosquitoes.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.940
It's a good time to be anywhere but here.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:22.660
The mosquitoes feed on anything in their path.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:30.000
Warm-blooded victims must sit and bear several miserable weeks of

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:45.550
stinging bites. But the mosquitoes are more than a misery. In such numbers, they can drain

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:56.480
enough blood to fell a caribou and sometimes even kill it. Caribou are forced off their

00:29:56.480 --> 00:30:00.000
grazing grounds by a predator 10 million times smaller than they.

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:08.800
The herds try to find refuge on the coldest patches, where mosquitoes will not venture.

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:23.680
But soon, hunger will force the caribou back to the grazing grounds with their clouds of tiny tormentors.

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:29.190
The spring thaw defrosts the Arctic.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:36.050
Far to the south, another vast region is transformed by its own springtime.

00:30:36.050 --> 00:30:41.230
In the mountains above the Amazon basin, melting snow creates a torrent of water unequalled

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:43.600
anywhere in the world.

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:47.810
The Andes Mountains rise to over 23,000 feet.

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:52.130
The world's longest continuous mountain range holds millions of tons of water locked in

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:53.130
its icy vaults.

00:30:53.130 --> 00:31:00.000
When the water is released, it cascades into the Amazon River.

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.350
and on into the greatest rainforest on Earth.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:30.000
Which habitat waits?

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:31.670
for the coming flood.

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:34.220
Nowhere is there

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:35.740
greater diversity of life

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:37.480
with only a small proportion

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:38.280
of its species

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:39.940
yet identified by scientists.

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:54.470
Now, before the flood,

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.290
the Amazon is at its lowest.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:07.330
The great egrets have been left with only small pools in which to fish.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:11.190
Their fortunes will rise and fall with the river's changing level,

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:16.770
because in the Amazon's shallows, the predatory piranha fish also await the flood.

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:44.000
As the water rises, everything changes.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:49.530
Treetops that stretch 30 feet above dry ground

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:53.780
are now almost submerged.

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:56.980
Dolphins swim among their branches.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:07.390
These are river dolphins, the Boto.

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:09.530
Utterly at home in the murky floodwaters,

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:22.200
the Boto's virtual blindness is no hindrance to its hunting.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:30.000
As forest and river meet,

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:39.810
emerge, a new abundance of prey comes within range of the region's hunters.

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:49.490
Some have extraordinary ways of getting to it.

00:33:49.490 --> 00:33:52.980
The arowana fish is also called the water monkey.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:13.000
It's at least three feet long and it can launch itself twice its body length to seize insects

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:27.040
from the branches overhead.

00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:29.670
On the dry islands that remain in the flooded forest,

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:37.650
miniature army is on the march with thousands of recruits a camp of army

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:42.960
ants is a moving bivouac its walls are live with tiny insects it's been three

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:49.660
weeks since they last set out on maneuvers the forests insect populations

00:34:49.660 --> 00:34:54.760
have had time to recover from the army's last life-or-death mission now once again

00:34:54.760 --> 00:35:00.000
the ants must find food for their own vast numbers they will attempt to devour any animal

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:00.860
in their path.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:05.290
700,000 soldiers stream

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:07.010
out across the forest floor in

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:09.010
columns that may stretch for nearly a mile.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:11.850
Scent trails laid down by the

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:13.570
ants' leaders mark the way forward.

00:35:22.210 --> 00:35:27.690
Tarantula hurries away from the scavenging

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:30.000
mob. It's overtaken by the

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:31.940
ants, the spider will have no chance

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:32.500
of survival.

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:40.890
Manages to scurry off,

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:45.910
but a huge scorpion falls victim

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:48.180
to the horde. These tiny warriors

00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:50.020
are simply too small to sting.

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:00.000
With any of its victims, the army paths

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:03.140
the carcass and carries it back behind the front lines.

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:06.600
The ants will, in turn, distribute the spoils of the hunt

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:09.800
to the queen and the colony's voracious larvae.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:18.270
Meanwhile, the egret's fortunes have risen with the river.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:28.780
Their chicks, three weeks old, are growing rapidly,

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:30.000
fed on the abundant land.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:36.170
supply of fish that is now close by the larder is overflowing their parents have

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:52.480
only to raid it but living so close to the water's surface means living closer

00:36:52.480 --> 00:37:00.000
and closer to some ferocious neighbors deadly piranhas lurk just below the

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:09.880
nests. Also patrolling

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:11.320
nearby is the Cayman,

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.130
the South American crocodile.

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:20.480
Just one week later, the water has risen

00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:22.870
even higher, and the egret chicks

00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:25.350
have grown taller. Even the simple

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:27.390
act of walking doesn't come easily for the rangy

00:37:27.390 --> 00:37:30.000
youngsters. They balance precarious

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:36.310
Staggering through the awkward stages of adolescence and sometimes making a crucial mistake

00:38:03.680 --> 00:38:06.300
Another young bird has fallen into the river.

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:08.320
Its feathers are waterlogged.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:29.120
This one manages to climb out of harm's way.

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:44.310
Still, the water creeps higher.

00:38:44.310 --> 00:38:46.550
Now the chicks totter above an entire pack

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:48.410
of horrific predators.

00:38:48.410 --> 00:38:51.580
With jaws even more efficient than the caimans,

00:38:51.580 --> 00:38:54.530
one false step will mean a feast for the piranhas

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:55.860
only a few feet away.

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:08.270
The first splash draws an entire school to a feeding frenzy.

00:39:08.270 --> 00:39:22.550
Teeth, like triangular razor blades, bring the drama to a swift end.

00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:27.080
The feast is over in less than 30 seconds.

00:39:34.760 --> 00:39:37.520
Extreme conditions provide for some hunters

00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:39.500
and challenge others.

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:41.940
Along the seashore, life is turned upside down

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:43.780
each and every day.

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:47.540
Tides, currents, wind and rough weather.

00:39:48.490 --> 00:39:53.190
The shoreline is an especially hostile home.

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:57.130
In Alaska, sea otters feed easily when the ocean is calm.

00:39:57.130 --> 00:39:59.800
Each eats about 10 pounds of seafood a day.

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:17.120
its body weight. To make a meal, an otter dives for a muscle or other shellfish. You must then take

00:40:17.120 --> 00:40:30.000
it to the surface to smash it open against a rock, tricky enough without waves crashing down. Between

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:45.560
In the waves, the otter's stomach makes a convenient workspace.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:55.350
But when autumn storms arrive, the coast becomes a tough place to catch a meal.

00:40:55.350 --> 00:40:59.220
Spring storms challenge the hunters of North America's eastern coast.

00:41:00.120 --> 00:41:04.200
Along the Grand Banks off Newfoundland, capelin are spawning.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:11.200
The fish are food for seabirds and seals.

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:24.110
Like the sea otters, the seals struggle to gain a meal in turbulent water.

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.560
But it's a challenge they cannot refuse.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:38.440
Many of nature's changes are daily events, others are far more rare.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:43.330
On this one small beach on the Pacific coast of Central America, a remarkable gathering

00:41:43.330 --> 00:41:47.300
takes place perhaps once in five years.

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:55.660
It is an Arabada, the arrival of tens of thousands of female olive ridley turtles.

00:41:55.660 --> 00:42:00.000
For this one crucial event, the turtles lumber up out of the waves prompted by their

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:06.010
own biological clocks and timed to phases of the moon. From out of the waves

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:19.050
and in waves the turtles come. The Arabada will create a glut, a seeming waste of

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:28.210
nature's resources but one for which there is good reason. At any one time

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:30.000
there may be several hundred turtles

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:36.840
the beach. Over the course of four months, there will be over 100,000 turtle nests and up to 10

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:44.210
million eggs on this one small beach. It's an irresistible draw for predators. They arrive from

00:42:44.210 --> 00:42:51.580
near and far to take advantage of the food supply they know is coming. The mother turtles are

00:42:51.580 --> 00:42:57.100
selective. Each checks the sand with her beak, searching for the less salty smell or the

00:42:57.100 --> 00:43:00.000
slightly higher temperature that tells her she's found the tides high.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:07.830
watermark. This may be the most important moment in the turtles' lives, preparing

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:22.330
for a generation to come. Each carries about 100 eggs. Although the mothers try

00:43:22.330 --> 00:43:26.010
to bury their eggs, some vultures move in even before the turtles have finished

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:30.000
laying. The beach is so crowded that some turtles dig up the

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:38.120
eggs laid by others, exposing them to the birds. The turtles fling sand over the eggs

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:53.490
as quickly as they can. It'll be six to seven weeks before the new generation hatches. With

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:59.510
most of the eggs buried, the vultures are frustrated. Until another predator arrives,

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:09.940
This relative of the raccoon comes running when the Arabada begins and quickly demonstrates

00:44:09.940 --> 00:44:12.850
why its name means "nosy".

00:44:12.850 --> 00:44:20.040
In a matter of seconds, the kawati mundi sniffs out the buried eggs.

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:38.500
As the adult turtles return to the sea,

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:53.900
they leave a new generation behind to fend for itself.

00:44:53.900 --> 00:44:57.710
For those turtles that hatch, it's a gritty start in life.

00:44:57.710 --> 00:45:00.000
Followed by one of the most perilous trips

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:08.380
young animal will ever attempt. They must run a gauntlet to the sea. With the laying

00:45:08.380 --> 00:45:12.280
so carefully timed to the high spring tides, the hatchlings have only to cross

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:29.280
the shortest possible distance. The vultures have been watching and waiting.

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:35.990
Even with their sharp vision and sharper beaks, they cannot clean out an entire nest no matter how fast they feed.

00:45:35.990 --> 00:45:47.670
The effort of laying so many eggs has paid off.

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:52.820
At least this hatchling survives the snapping beaks to join others on the next leg of the trek.

00:46:10.140 --> 00:46:15.140
Everything is an obstacle to these tiny turtles, and there are more predators up ahead.

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:30.000
Ghost crabs emerge from their burrows.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:32.000
makes a delectable treat.

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:51.400
This one is unwilling to oblige.

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:57.060
For the turtles that have made it this far,

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:58.060
the waves are in sight.

00:46:58.060 --> 00:47:00.000
But now yet another

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:05.200
predator joins in the hunt. Hundreds of frigate birds able to pick off baby

00:47:05.200 --> 00:47:23.140
turtles while on the wing. Just as some of the hatchlings reach the water, they are snatched.

00:47:31.470 --> 00:47:34.260
The carnage is part of a strategic plan.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:37.570
By providing such numbers of potential offspring,

00:47:37.570 --> 00:47:41.210
nature has assured that at least a few will survive.

00:47:41.210 --> 00:47:46.110
From a nest of 100, one or two will return from the sea

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:54.650
to breed at this very same beach.

00:47:54.650 --> 00:48:00.000
This hatchling has made it to the relative safety

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:06.640
of its new world.

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:10.100
Of the great feasts that take place all across the planet,

00:48:10.100 --> 00:48:13.240
none is harder to predict than the one about to unfold here

00:48:13.240 --> 00:48:16.880
in the South African desert of Namakwa land,

00:48:16.880 --> 00:48:20.160
where it's been 20 years since rain last fell.

00:48:31.180 --> 00:48:35.510
Finally, clouds move in, and a deluge begins.

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:47.430
For the animals of this dry and dusty place,

00:48:47.430 --> 00:48:51.660
there's suddenly enough water to quench any thirst.

00:48:51.660 --> 00:48:57.440
More than enough for those who normally depend

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:00.000
on the tiniest droplets of moisture to survive.

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:16.400
As the clouds give way to sunshine, the desert is transformed.

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:26.370
From long-stored seeds, more than 4,000 kinds of flowering plants coat the desert in color.

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:49.650
And when the rains trigger the plants to come to life,

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:54.380
something else comes to life nearby.

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:56.310
Plant eaters.

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:05.500
Now the ground itself seems alive with locusts.

00:50:05.500 --> 00:50:10.840
Their eggs have lain dormant in suspended animation for up to 60 years.

00:50:10.840 --> 00:50:16.500
The locusts are close relatives of the much larger grasshopper.

00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:24.900
A generation matures in just two weeks, but only if the ground is damp can the locusts lay their eggs and breed by the billion.

00:50:36.880 --> 00:51:00.000
50 billion munching mouths, it's the greatest feast anywhere on earth. The locust

00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:11.010
grow they molt and then develop wings now they're even more mobile as they strip miles of vegetation

00:51:11.010 --> 00:51:19.940
the locusts making no distinction between natural growth and cultivated crops any such gathering

00:51:19.940 --> 00:51:25.660
inevitably draws its own predators wattled starlings grab up beakfuls of locusts and feed

00:51:25.660 --> 00:51:26.900
them to their hungry chicks

00:51:40.430 --> 00:51:43.550
It's no wonder the starlings are also known as locust birds.

00:51:47.590 --> 00:51:49.490
European storks have also gathered.

00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:54.030
They're a long way from their nesting sites, but they've come upon an unbelievable banquet.

00:52:08.350 --> 00:52:13.600
Yet no matter how many locusts the birds eat, the onslaught will not be stopped.

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:18.010
Only when they have consumed every possible fruit source in their path will the destruction

00:52:18.010 --> 00:52:22.900
come to a natural end.

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:25.210
Spring turns to summer.

00:52:25.210 --> 00:52:28.710
Africa shimmers in the heat.

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:30.000
The rains have not yet arrived.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:33.630
in the grassy savanna, the lion's domain.

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:37.330
It's been six months since they feasted on herds of zebra.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:42.500
They face a stark choice: to take greater risks out on the grasslands

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:47.990
or settle for less attractive prey they can find nearby.

00:52:47.990 --> 00:52:57.900
Warthog. Small consolation for a ravenous stomach.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:00.000
But its pursuit requires stealth,

00:53:00.220 --> 00:53:00.900
and caution

00:53:00.900 --> 00:53:03.350
the warthog's tusks

00:53:03.350 --> 00:53:04.530
can gore a lion

00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:11.420
they check their positions

00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:12.620
staying downwind

00:53:12.620 --> 00:53:13.560
as long as possible

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:14.960
intent on their prey

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:30.000
the warthogs do not sense

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:33.200
the lions, although they are perfectly positioned to attack.

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:29.190
Surprise! Only a piglet, just a few mouthfuls, for just one lion.

00:54:32.750 --> 00:54:34.670
The pride will soon have to hunt again.

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:37.870
The group desperately needs larger prey.

00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:44.470
The gamble to stay near the oasis will pay off.

00:54:45.250 --> 00:54:47.730
It's the only patch of rich grass left in the savannah,

00:54:48.170 --> 00:54:50.640
and it begins to draw animals from miles around.

00:54:51.940 --> 00:54:54.760
A huge herd of buffalo will make the greatest challenge of all,

00:54:55.620 --> 00:54:58.300
and the greatest reward if the lions are successful.

00:55:05.790 --> 00:55:12.590
One buffalo can feed an entire pride, but it is also four times a lion's size.

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:16.450
Few animals on earth will tackle an animal larger than themselves.

00:55:16.450 --> 00:55:19.680
Only teamwork will give the lion a chance of success.

00:55:44.110 --> 00:55:48.080
The ride has selected a pair of buffalo, oblivious to their approach.

00:55:48.080 --> 00:55:56.880
With dry grass, they move carefully onto the marsh.

00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:05.870
Only a final moment remains before the ultimate test of every muscle, tooth, and claw.

00:56:07.870 --> 00:56:09.230
The outcome is far from certain.

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:12.420
Skill and luck must meet in mere moments.

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:42.540
One of the lions discovers the true price of the hunt.

00:56:42.540 --> 00:56:44.120
She is mortally wounded.

00:57:00.860 --> 00:57:05.900
It takes four lions to bring the buffalo to the ground where it finally yields to their

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:08.000
suffocating embrace.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:32.940
Death-defying risk has ended in a feast,

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:36.990
and life for all but one of the pride.

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:45.860
From tropical forest to the frozen tundra,

00:57:45.860 --> 00:57:48.220
nature serves up countless variations

00:57:48.220 --> 00:57:52.770
on one potent theme, the need to find food.

00:57:52.770 --> 00:57:54.640
Virtually every animal hunts,

00:57:54.640 --> 00:57:57.860
or finds itself among the hunted.

00:57:57.860 --> 00:58:00.000
Teamwork, the solitary prowl,

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:02.800
Outright theft.

00:58:02.800 --> 00:58:05.800
Animals will pursue any strategy to snare another meal,

00:58:05.800 --> 00:58:07.720
no matter the risk.

00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:10.370
But whatever the tactics,

00:58:10.370 --> 00:58:12.670
when hunters meet the hunted,

00:58:12.670 --> 00:58:14.670
all share the same instinct:

00:58:14.670 --> 00:58:17.450
a will to survive.
